Management also wasn't stingy about the stuff that mattered. Overtime got paid properly, not the sketchy "we'll make it up to you" version some places do. When a driver got into a minor accident, the company covered everything and didn't dock his pay while he recovered, which I know isn't universal in this industry and I don't take it for granted.

I ended up leaving because my partner got a job offer in another city and honestly the commute plus the shift hours plus a long distance situation just wasn't sustainable long term. It wasn't the job pushing me out, it was just life logistics beating actual logistics, ironic given the industry.

