I worked coordination and logistics for an agricultural co-op for close to two years, right after finishing school, and this is one of those jobs I left not because it was bad but because I outgrew what it could offer me, which somehow makes it harder to write about than a job I hated.

My role was mostly administrative and logistical, helping coordinate between member farmers, managing the harvest schedules, dealing with buyers, tracking which fields were producing what and when trucks needed to show up to move product before it spoiled. It's a job with real stakes attached to it in a way office jobs rarely have, because if you mess up a pickup schedule, actual food rots, actual farmers lose actual income they were counting on, and that weight sat with me the whole time, in a mostly good way, the kind that makes you take your work seriously.

The people were the best part, honestly. Co-op culture is fundamentally different from corporate culture because everyone genuinely has skin in the game together, farmers whose families had worked the same land for generations, sharing equipment, covering for each other during sickness or bad weather without anyone keeping score. I got invited to actual family meals during harvest season, not as a token gesture but because that's just how the community worked, you show up, you help, you eat together after.

Weather was the constant stress though, the thing no amount of good planning could fully control. I remember one season where a storm came in two days before a major harvest window and everyone, me included, was out coordinating emergency picks at odd hours, drenched, exhausted, watching months of work hang on a weather forecast that kept changing. We saved most of it that year. Some years the co-op didn't, and watching farmers absorb that loss stoically, already planning next season instead of dwelling, taught me something about resilience I don't think I'll unlearn.

Pay honestly wasn't great, co-ops don't have deep margins to pay administrative staff much above modest, and there wasn't much room to grow into a bigger role since the org itself was small and mostly farmer-led by design. I left for a position in agricultural supply chain at a larger company, better pay, more structured career path, the practical adult reasons people give when a place they loved just can't offer what they need long-term.

I still think about harvest season more than I expected to, the specific exhausted satisfaction of a good pickup day, trucks loaded, everyone tired and joking around at the end of it. Would I recommend working at a co-op. Genuinely yes, especially early in your career if you want to learn what real stakes and real community feel like in work. Just know the ceiling on pay and growth is real, and go in knowing you might, like me, eventually need to leave for practical reasons even while missing it.
